> Well, there are no worthless glass rings, and I think it is not too far
> fetched that the character knows this is the case. Also, presumably stones
> in rings are cut, while those found loose are in their raw form, since you
> can sometimes just dig them out of the stone. Thus it would be hard to tell
> worthless glass from a raw gemstome by knowing how a cut gemstone looks.

   I totally agree with Ohle on the rationale here. Uncut gems look
nothing like set gems; the current behavior makes perfect sense.
   Nevertheless, because this sounded like an interesting patch, I went
ahead and made a first-cut patch for anyone who /does/ want to use it. 
It's here:
http://www.contrib.andrew.cmu.edu/~ajo/free-software/nethack/unidring.diff

   It handles the bare-bones request (make un-ID'ed gem rings show up
as "(color) stone ring"), and also hacks up the wishing code to let
you wish for "a green stone ring" etc., which will give you either an
emerald ring or a jade ring at random, and so on.

   That's the first half of the patch. The second half --- unwritten
and I think harder --- would be to do something intelligent:

   * when the player IDs a ring with "identify". The Right Thing is
probably to ID the corresponding gemstone: not too hard.

   * when the player IDs a ring by its effects: The Right Thing is
to ID the ring, but not the gemstone, and force '\' to display
"gain strength (green stone)" until the gem was ID'ed.

I may work on the second half this evening, or I may just let it be.

